    <span class="eyebrow">For the Counter</span>
    <h1>Something worth handing over.</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">I kept losing money at the kitchen table &mdash; tired, customer waiting, and the number I said in the next thirty seconds was the one I had to honor in the morning. So I built a quoting tool for that moment.</p>

    <p>I'm a plumber. I've run a plumbing and heating shop for years, and I built Dead Hand for my own truck first &mdash; because I kept talking myself into a number I regretted by the time I wrote the invoice.</p>

    <p>You know the guys I mean. One truck, maybe three. Good at the work. They price in their head and most of them are better at it than they think, but not at seven on a Friday night. Some of them are underwater on jobs they would swear made money.</p>

    <p>Dead Hand fixes the number, not the man. They set their labor rates, their material margin and their overhead once. After that they put in the hours and the parts and the right price comes out with the margin already inside it. Then they turn the phone around and the customer gets a clean estimate with none of that on it. Fifteen a month. It runs in a browser, so there is no app store and nothing to install on whatever phone they already carry.</p>


