    <h2>Built for the small shop, not the enterprise.</h2>

    <p>The big field-service platforms are built for a company with a dispatcher, an office and an onboarding budget. That's a real business and they serve it well. It just isn't the man at your counter with one truck and a helper.</p>

    <p><strong>I'm not trying to get in the truck with everyone who works for every company. I'm trying to get in the truck with the ones it will actually help.</strong> That's why it's priced by the size of the shop instead of by the head, and why there's no implementation package. A one-man shop doesn't need onboarding. He needs the right number at the kitchen table.</p>

    <h2>What it does that the flat-rate books don't</h2>

    <div class="dl">
      <div><b>The numbers are his.</b> The flat-rate books sell a maintained price list and update it every quarter. Dead Hand ships the shape of a job with every price blank &mdash; his town, his overhead, his payroll, not somebody else's.</div>
      <div><b>He isn't charged by the head.</b> Per-tech pricing costs a shop more every time it hires. This is flat by the size of the shop.</div>
      <div><b>Nothing to set up.</b> No implementation fee, no onboarding package, no consultant sitting in his kitchen.</div>
      <div><b>The price is on the website.</b> Nobody has to sit through a demo to find out what it costs.</div>
      <div><b>Margin on every line while he builds the price</b> &mdash; before he says the number, not after the job is done.</div>
    </div>

    <p><strong>$15 a month</strong> for the calculator, or <strong>$30 to $80</strong> flat by shop size for the whole book of jobs priced and ready. The cheapest per-tech book runs more than twice the calculator for a single man, plus a few hundred dollars before he quotes anything.</p>

    <h2>Why it's worth the counter space</h2>

    <p>You carry these guys on terms. <strong>The ones who guess low are the ones who quietly disappear owing you money</strong> &mdash; not because they're bad at the work, but because they priced a job in their head at seven on a Friday and ate the difference all year.</p>

    <p>Someone doing better work and making more money is a better customer for you. That's the whole argument. A shop that prices right stays on the books, keeps the truck full, and keeps buying.</p>

    <h2>No spiff, and that's on purpose.</h2>

    <p>I'm not offering you a cut. Plenty of houses don't let their people take money from a vendor, and I'm not going to hand you something you'd have to declare or turn down.</p>

    

    <h2>If you want to point someone at it</h2>

    <p>Tell them <strong>getdeadhand.com</strong>, or point them at the code on the sheet below. It opens <strong>dead-hand.app/demo</strong> &mdash; a real water heater job, already priced, that they can change and re-price on their own phone. No sign-up, no form for you to fill in, and nothing is saved off their phone.</p>

    <div class="say">
      <span class="lbl">If it helps, the sentence that works</span>
      <p>&ldquo;There&rsquo;s a plumber up in PA who built a quoting app for guys like you. Scan that sheet &mdash; it&rsquo;ll price a water heater on your phone. Fifteen a month.&rdquo;</p>
    </div>

    <p>There&rsquo;s a sheet below you can print and stick on the counter or the board. The code on it opens the working quote on their phone camera &mdash; no app, no typing, no account.</p>

    <div class="grabs">
